WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.463
Federal AI policy was rewritten in 2025.

2
00:00:03.463 --> 00:00:08.943
OMB memo M twenty-five twenty-one governs
use: a Chief AI Officer, an annual

3
00:00:08.943 --> 00:00:11.774
use-case inventory, compliance plans,

4
00:00:11.774 --> 00:00:15.337
and seven minimum practices
for high-impact AI.

5
00:00:15.337 --> 00:00:20.465
Its companion, M twenty-five twenty-two,
governs acquisition, and says AI operated

6
00:00:20.465 --> 00:00:25.975
as an information system must receive an
authorization to operate before deployment.

7
00:00:25.975 --> 00:00:28.250
What the rules don't do matters as much.

8
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.788
There is no AI-specific authorization.

9
00:00:30.788 --> 00:00:35.956
Agencies must keep following Circular
A-130 for AI systems, and the memo's own

10
00:00:35.956 --> 00:00:41.288
risk acceptance is separate from, and
doesn't supersede, system authorization.

11
00:00:41.288 --> 00:00:43.713
It sets no identity requirements at all.

12
00:00:43.713 --> 00:00:47.442
So a high-impact use case needs
two decisions before it deploys:

13
00:00:47.442 --> 00:00:52.462
the signed risk acceptance on its impact
assessment, and the system's authorization.

14
00:00:52.462 --> 00:00:53.487
One more note.

15
00:00:53.487 --> 00:00:57.253
Executive Order fourteen four
thirty-four tells agencies

16
00:00:57.253 --> 00:01:00.712
to say Super Intelligence
in non-statutory documents.

17
00:01:00.712 --> 00:01:04.359
The definition hasn't changed,
and the book keeps the term AI,

18
00:01:04.359 --> 00:01:07.390
because the instruments
it applies still use it.
